"{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.3765/exabs.v0i0.576\",\n  \"title\": \"Corpora of Non-Linguistic Symbol Systems\",\n  \"authors\": \"Katherine Wu; Jennifer Solman; Ruth Linehan; Richard Sproat\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>The popular press has promoted recent claims of statistical methods that can distinguish writing from non-linguistic symbols. One problem with such claims, though, is the dearth of non-linguistic symbol \\\"texts\\\" which could be compared with written language. This project fills that void by developing electronic corpora of known non-linguistic systems. To date we have developed corpora of several systems: heraldry; Totem poles; Mesopotamian deity symbols; Vinča symbols; Pictish symbols; mathematical equations; weather icons. Corpus sizes range from several hundred to several tens of thousands of symbols. All corpora are encoded in XML and will be released under an open-source license.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.3765/exabs.v0i0.576\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2012-04-08T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"http://journals.linguisticsociety.org/proceedings/index.php/ExtendedAbs/article/viewFile/576/487\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.3765/exabs.v0i0.576\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 1,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Linguistic Society of America', 'container_title': 'LSA Annual Meeting Extended Abstracts', 'volume': '3', 'issue': '', 'page': '4', 'issn': ['2377-3367'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '2085', 'prefix': '10.3765'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1007/978-3-031-26809-0_3\",\n  \"title\": \"A Taxonomy of Non-linguistic Symbol Systems\",\n  \"authors\": \"Richard Sproat\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1007/978-3-031-26809-0_3\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2023-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-26809-0_3\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Springer Nature Switzerland', 'container_title': 'Symbols', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '21-89', 'issn': [], 'isbn': ['9783031268083', '9783031268090'], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '297', 'prefix': '10.1007'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1353/lan.2014.0031\",\n  \"title\": \"A Statistical Comparison of Written Language and Nonlinguistic Symbol\\n                        Systems\",\n  \"authors\": \"Richard Sproat\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>Are statistical methods useful in distinguishing written language from\\n                        nonlinguistic symbol systems? Some recent articles (Rao et al. 2009a, Lee et\\n                        al. 2010a) have claimed so. Both of these previous articles use measures\\n                        based at least in part on bigram conditional entropy, and subsequent work by\\n                        one of the authors (Rao) has used other entropic measures. In both cases the\\n                        authors have argued that the methods proposed either are useful for\\n                        discriminating between linguistic and nonlinguistic systems (Lee et al.), or\\n                        at least count as evidence of a more ‘inductive’ kind for the status of a\\n                        system (Rao et al.).</jats:p>\\n                  <jats:p>Using a larger set of nonlinguistic and comparison linguistic corpora\\n                        than were used in these and other studies, I show that none of the\\n                        previously proposed methods are useful as published. However, one of the\\n                        measures proposed by Lee and colleagues (2010a) (with a different cut-off\\n                        value) and a novel measure based on repetition turn out to be good measures\\n                        for classifying symbol systems into the two categories. For the two ancient\\n                        symbol systems of interest to Rao and colleagues (2009a) and Lee and\\n                        colleagues (2010a)—Indus Valley inscriptions and Pictish symbols,\\n                        respectively—both of these measures classify them as nonlinguistic,\\n                        contradicting the findings of those previous works.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1353/lan.2014.0031\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2014-06-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1353/lan.2014.0031\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 19,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Cambridge University Press (CUP)', 'container_title': 'Language', 'volume': '90', 'issue': '2', 'page': '457-481', 'issn': ['0097-8507', '1535-0665'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '56', 'prefix': '10.1017'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1075/wll.16.1.06ung\",\n  \"title\": \"Zipf and morphemes\",\n  \"authors\": \"J. Marshall Unger\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1075/wll.16.1.06ung\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2013-03-08T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1075/wll.16.1.06ung\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'John Benjamins Publishing Company', 'container_title': 'Written Language &amp; Literacy', 'volume': '16', 'issue': '1', 'page': '112-114', 'issn': ['1387-6732', '1570-6001'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '1757', 'prefix': '10.1075'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1093/oso/9780195159769.003.0025\",\n  \"title\": \"A Generalization of the Zipf-Mandelbrot Law in Linguistics\",\n  \"authors\": \"Marcelo A. Montemurro\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<p>Human language evolved by natural mechanisms into an efficient system capable of coding and transmitting highly structured information [12, 13, 14]. As a remarkable complex system it allows many levels of description across its organizational hierarchy [1, 11, 18]. In this context statistical analysis stands as a valuable tool in order to reveal robust structural patterns that may have resulted from its long evolutionary history. In this chapter we shall address the statistical regularities of human language at its most basic level of description, namely the rank-frequency distribution of words. Around 1932 the philologist George Zipf [6, 19, 20] noted the manifestation of several robust power-law distributions arising in different realms of human activity. Among them, the most striking was undoubtedly the one referring to the distribution of words frequencies in human languages. The best way to introduce Zipf's law for words is by means of a concrete example. Let us take a literary work, say, James Joyce's Ulysses, and perform some basic statistics on it, whic simply consists in counting all the words present in the text and noting how many occurrences each distinct word form has. For this particular text we should arrive at the following numbers: the total number of words N = 268,112, and the number of different word forms V = 28,838. We can now order the list of different words according to decreasing number of occurrences, and we can assign to each word a rank index s equal to its position in the list starting from the most frequent word. Some general features of the rank-ordered list of words can be mentioned at this point. First, the top-rank words are functional components of language devoid of direct meaning, such as the article the and prepositions, for instance. A few ranks down the list, words more related to the contents of the text start to appear. </p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1093/oso/9780195159769.003.0025\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2004-04-15T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780195159769.003.0025\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 4,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Oxford University Press', 'container_title': 'Nonextensive Entropy', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': ['9780195159769', '9780197562024'], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '286', 'prefix': '10.1093'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1007/978-3-031-72193-9\",\n  \"title\": \"Introduction to Writing Systems\",\n  \"authors\": \"Richard Sproat\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1007/978-3-031-72193-9\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2024-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-72193-9.pdf\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-72193-9\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Springer Nature Switzerland', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': ['9783031721939'], 'crossref_type': 'book', 'member': '297', 'prefix': '10.1007'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1007/978-3-031-72193-9_7\",\n  \"title\": \"History of writing systems\",\n  \"authors\": \"Richard Sproat\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1007/978-3-031-72193-9_7\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2024-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-72193-9_7\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Springer Nature Switzerland', 'container_title': 'Introduction to Writing Systems', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': ['9783031721939'], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '297', 'prefix': '10.1007'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1007/978-3-031-72193-9_8\",\n  \"title\": \"Summary\",\n  \"authors\": \"Richard Sproat\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1007/978-3-031-72193-9_8\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2024-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-72193-9_8\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Springer Nature Switzerland', 'container_title': 'Introduction to Writing Systems', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': ['9783031721939'], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '297', 'prefix': '10.1007'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1080/01969720902847029\",\n  \"title\": \"INTERPRETATION OF THE GENERALIZED ZIPF-MANDELBROT LAW PARAMETERS\",\n  \"authors\": \"Patricia Sastre-Vázquez; Yolanda Villacampa; José A. Reyes; Fernando García-Alonso\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1080/01969720902847029\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2009-04-28T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1080/01969720902847029\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 2,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Informa UK Limited', 'container_title': 'Cybernetics and Systems', 'volume': '40', 'issue': '4', 'page': '326-336', 'issn': ['0196-9722', '1087-6553'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '301', 'prefix': '10.1080'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1007/978-3-031-72193-9_1\",\n  \"title\": \"Preliminaries\",\n  \"authors\": \"Richard Sproat\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1007/978-3-031-72193-9_1\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2024-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://d